  "slug": "assembly-topic-2af3",
  "summary": "Recent in situ, structural, and in vivo studies resolve how multisubunit assemblies remodel membranes in native and developmental contexts.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the formation of defined multisubunit assemblies that physically shape membranes. In situ cryo-electron tomography of diatom mitochondria resolves native respiratory supercomplexes, including an oxphosome, alongside ATP synthase organization. Cryo-EM of the E. coli flotillin YqiK reveals a 54-subunit membrane cage built from long wall helices, showing how bacterial flotillins construct membrane scaffolds. In plant development, F-actin rings encircle spherical vacuoles and, through the crosslinker SCAB1, tether them to central vacuoles to promote homotypic fusion. In C. elegans epidermis, a persistent ESCRT-III assembly at large plasma membrane wounds restores membrane tension and supports repair. Together these reports link structural architecture to membrane function across organelle, bacterial, and cell-wide scales.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
